introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we solve a real client's problem with code?"
  overview: "The <strong>Internal Assessment (IA)</strong> is your opportunity to behave like a professional developer. You will identify a client with a real problem, design a solution, build it using complex techniques, and evaluate its success. This is not just about coding — it's about the entire <strong>Cycle of Development</strong>."
  journey: "You will follow the 5 Criteria: <strong>Planning (A)</strong> to define requirements, <strong>Design (B)</strong> to blueprint the system, <strong>Development (C)</strong> to prove your coding skills, <strong>Functionality (D)</strong> to demonstrate the product, and <strong>Evaluation (E)</strong> to reflect on the outcome."

concepts:
  - title: "Criterion A: Planning"
    icon: "fa-clipboard-list"
    content: |
      <p>Everything starts with a <strong>Problem Specification</strong>. You must:</p>
      <ul>
          <li>Identify a real <strong>Client</strong> (Stakeholder).</li>
          <li>Define the scenario and why a digital solution is needed.</li>
          <li>List specific, measurable <strong>Success Criteria</strong> (e.g., "The system shall calculate averages in under 2 seconds").</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Criterion B: Solution Overview"
    icon: "fa-compass-drafting"
    content: |
      <p>Before coding, you plan. This criterion covers:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Record of Tasks:</strong> A chronology of your work (e.g., a GANTT chart).</li>
          <li><strong>Design Overview:</strong> Flowcharts, UML diagrams, and UI wireframes that blueprint your solution.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Criterion C: Development"
    icon: "fa-code"
    content: |
      <p>This is the "Complexity" criterion. You must demonstrate high-level coding skills (e.g., Sorting, Searching, File I/O, OOP) and document <em>how</em> you implemented them. It's not about the whole code, but the <strong>ingenious parts</strong>.</p>

  - title: "Criterion D: Functionality"
    icon: "fa-video"
    content: |
      <p>Does it work? You will produce a <strong>Video</strong> (max 7 minutes) demonstrating the features defined in Criterion A. The examiner assesses the product based on this video.</p>

terminology:
  - term: "Internal Assessment (IA)"
    definition: "A significant project (20% of grade) where students develop a software solution for a client."
  - term: "Stakeholder (Client)"
    definition: "The person who will use the product. You must consult them throughout the process."
  - term: "Success Criteria"
    definition: "A list of specific, measurable requirements that the final product must meet."
  - term: "Record of Tasks"
    definition: "A chronological list (often a GANTT chart) showing the planning, design, dev, and testing stages."
  - term: "Prototype"
    definition: "An early model of the product used to get feedback from the client."
  - term: "Complexity"
    definition: "The level of technical difficulty in the code (e.g., using nested loops, custom classes, or recursion)."
  - term: "Evaluation"
    definition: "Critically assessing whether the solution met the success criteria and suggesting future improvements."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Code is Everything"
    correction: "Thinking the IA is just a large coding task. The Documentation (planning, design, eval) is worth more than half the marks!"
  - mistake: "Generic Projects"
    correction: "Building a generic 'Calculator' or 'Quiz' without a real client. You need a specific scenario (e.g., 'A specific teacher's Quiz system')."
  - mistake: "Templates are Innovation"
    correction: "Downloading a template and changing the colors. You receive zero marks for code you didn't write."

